00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:58.360
OK, so we got, like,
four or five people.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:00:59.980
I'm sorry to you guys.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.792
You can help me
out if I get stuck.

00:01:01.792 --> 00:01:03.750
For the rest of you, what
I'm going to do first

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:04.900
is basically for
your understanding

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:05.970
of a deep neural network.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.511
Just one of the
things that you might

00:01:07.511 --> 00:01:10.060
want to do at TensorFlow,
which is really good at.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.110
So the basics.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:16.950
This is taken from a
CodeLab, which we provide,

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:18.130
which is available on our site.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:19.880
So there will be a
slide in a moment which

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.569
you can all take photos of
with a link and everything.

00:01:22.569 --> 00:01:24.110
But we're going to
look at a problem,

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:27.435
and that's MNIST recognizing
the handwritten digits.

00:01:27.435 --> 00:01:29.060
And we're going to
look at how we could

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:30.600
do that in the most simple way.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.560
So we're not going to go
for the fully convolutional

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.470
to make the 99% accuracy.

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:39.112
We're going to look at
the basic neural network

00:01:39.112 --> 00:01:41.570
that we could make to actually
give us an understanding how

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:44.290
neural networks work.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.040
So the first thing
that we're going to do,

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:47.335
we've got two layers here.

00:01:47.335 --> 00:01:49.710
So we have the input layer,
and we have the output layer.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:53.230
So this is a pretty
simple neural network.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:56.656
It would be rude to call
it deep neural network.

00:01:56.656 --> 00:01:58.530
And basically what we're
doing, those images,

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.800
those handwritten images that we
were looking at, they are all,

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.534
let's say, 28 by 28 pixels.

00:02:03.534 --> 00:02:05.450
So if you stretch them
out into a long vector,

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:09.229
you get 784 pixels.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:12.630
And what we're going to try
to do is use those 784 pixels.

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.760
We're going to map them to
each one on our neurons.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.385
We're going to get
an output, and we're

00:02:16.385 --> 00:02:18.320
going to try and predict
which number it is.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.060
So the only information
we really have

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.150
is about the color, the
grayscale of each one

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:25.179
of those pixels coming in.

00:02:25.179 --> 00:02:26.970
So ignore the function
here for the moment.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:29.340
We'll come back to
that in a moment.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:33.410
So the first thing I want to
emphasize is this X and W here.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.690
So those pixels that we
just looked at coming in,

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:37.856
there are two things that
that neuron's going to do.

00:02:37.856 --> 00:02:41.236
It's going to do the weight
and it's going to do the input.

00:02:41.236 --> 00:02:42.860
And the weight is
basically-- it's just

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:46.490
the thing that makes the
whole neural network learn.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:51.410
We're going to emphasize or give
less attention to that weight

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:54.320
if we find that that
feature is significant.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.570
So when we're looking at
one of those neurons--

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.680
sorry for flickering
back and forward now--

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.890
all of those inputs are
coming into each neuron.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:06.877
And we will have a matrix of
weights which corresponds.

00:03:06.877 --> 00:03:08.960
And the first thing we
would do is a weighted sum.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:13.190
So we will take the
value from the pixel.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:17.030
So let's say we put it on a
scale of 0 to 255 or a decimal,

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.110
whatever you want.

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:20.180
And we will take a weight,
we multiply those two,

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:24.440
and we'll add them all up
for each one those neurons.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.900
There's an extra thing
here called the bias.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:31.286
That bias is, basically,
if you think about it,

00:03:31.286 --> 00:03:32.930
if you go back to
math class, you

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:35.000
had like a y-axis
and the x-axis.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.610
The bias is just basically where
it intercepts on the y-axis.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:40.020
So we'll leave that for now.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.570
But that's what it does.

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.830
OK, so in the previous
slide, what I showed here

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:49.804
is the softmax function.

00:03:49.804 --> 00:03:52.220
The softmax function we will
come back to in a little bit,

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:55.850
but because we don't have
multiple layers in our network,

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:57.170
we want to do some activation.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.560
But at the same time, we
want to work out probability.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:01.350
So softmax has these
nice properties

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.730
that will actually
take our scores,

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.930
like that value of
that weighted sum,

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:09.380
and turn it into a
probability for us.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.570
If we were to add
more layers, which

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:14.410
is what we're going to do
here, and this is, well,

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.410
as it states, it's overkill.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.510
It's not how you should
tackle this problem.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:21.140
But just to give you an
understanding of how you could

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.210
tackle this problem,
what we're doing now

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.540
is adding more layers
to our neural network.

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:28.760
So the idea of deep,
having multiple layers

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:29.919
comes into play.

00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:31.460
And in each one of
these layers we're

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:32.610
going to do the same thing.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.450
We're going to take
the value, the input.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:36.680
We're going to add a weight.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:37.880
We're going to do
that multiplication,

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:39.504
and we're going to
sum it up, and we'll

00:04:39.504 --> 00:04:43.160
pass it through to the next
one, and so on and so forth.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.260
Inside each of
those neurons, we're

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.050
going to have an
activation function.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:51.620
One function that you
might use is sigmoid.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.240
Lights just changed.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:56.540
OK, what does an
activation function do?

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:57.455
What's it all about?

00:04:57.455 --> 00:04:58.580
So let's have a look ahead.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.830
At the top row here, these
are all the different kinds

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:02.930
of, like they say, the
common activation function.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:05.660
The most common
activation function, well,

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:07.850
let's say the most easy
activation function, not

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:10.080
the least and the most common,
but the most easy is identity.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.750
You know, what you
put in, you get out.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:13.503
So you put in 10,
you get back 10.

00:05:13.503 --> 00:05:15.800
You put in minus
10, you get back 10.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:17.690
If we look at sigmoid,
so it's basically

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:19.295
mapping in between
space of 0 to 1.

00:05:19.295 --> 00:05:21.170
And it's kind of saying,
well, actually we're

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:22.211
going to squash it a bit.

00:05:22.211 --> 00:05:25.380
Notices the values at the
tail end or at the front,

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.112
they actually are going to
kind of get to the same value.

00:05:28.112 --> 00:05:29.570
And the stuff that's
in the middle,

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:31.403
we're going to kind of
emphasize a bit more.

00:05:31.403 --> 00:05:33.290
That's closer to the identity.

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:37.107
A lot of neural networks
actually use sigmoid.

00:05:37.107 --> 00:05:38.690
What we've actually
found with imaging

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:41.370
is ReLu, which is
this function here,

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:43.010
which is almost the
same as identity,

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:44.509
except for anything
that's negative,

00:05:44.509 --> 00:05:46.280
we're just going to put to zero.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.550
It's a function that works
quite well in that space.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:52.580
The reason why that
one works well--

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:54.590
so how it was
explained to me, it

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:58.070
was explained to me that
if you look at sigmoid,

00:05:58.070 --> 00:05:59.990
that's how we kind of
interpreted the world.

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:01.940
But when they started
to look at some

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:04.080
of the biological
functions of eye cells

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.080
and this kind of stuff,
they saw that the signal

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:10.760
that was actually coming
through was more akin to this.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:13.130
So they thought well,
let's give it a try.

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:13.849
And they did.

00:06:13.849 --> 00:06:15.640
And it seems to work
quite well in imaging.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:17.473
And you can obviously
think for the features

00:06:17.473 --> 00:06:19.520
that you're trying to
get, you're not losing

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:21.350
or you're not kind
of, let's say,

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:24.647
making that information at the
front end all flat anymore.

00:06:24.647 --> 00:06:26.730
So you're not bringing it
all into the same space.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:29.480
You're not losing that emphasis.

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:31.670
I think that the better
explanation I heard was they

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:33.410
tried it, it seemed
to work well,

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:37.290
and that was good to go further.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:39.230
The softmax function
at the bottom,

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:40.890
basically this is
a function that's

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:43.370
going to allow us to take
our scores or our logits,

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:45.578
and we're going to do
something to actually turn that

00:06:45.578 --> 00:06:46.620
into probabilities.

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:48.140
So this is a good
function to have

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:52.730
at the end to use in a
classification space.

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:58.150
So we've got our scores,
which is the top row.

00:06:58.150 --> 00:06:59.570
And we've got our--

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:03.080
sorry, we've got our scores,
which is the bottom row.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:05.987
I'm going to say this around
the wrong way again, aren't I?

00:07:05.987 --> 00:07:07.820
We have our scores,
which is the bottom row.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:09.350
And what we want
to compare it to

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:11.095
is what the actual values are.

00:07:11.095 --> 00:07:13.470
But the actual value in this
case is a handwritten digit,

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:15.590
so it's anywhere
between 0 and 9.

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:16.980
So we want to compare those.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:19.271
So we're going to do something
called one hot encoding.

00:07:19.271 --> 00:07:22.290
So there's basically, a 6
becomes a load of zeros and a 1

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:25.374
and then a few zeros, a
5, well, you can imagine.

00:07:25.374 --> 00:07:27.290
You can just look at
this and you can see why.

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:29.800
So 9 is going to be 000,
and then 1 at the end,

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.650
and 0 is going to be
one on a tail end of 9s.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:35.210
And then what we can do is
actually take the actual value

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:38.180
and we can take
the probabilities,

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:41.990
and we can work out the
distance between those two.

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:45.915
In neural networks, a popular
function is cross-entropy.

00:07:45.915 --> 00:07:49.010
What cross-entropy is basically
looking at is how much noise

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:52.850
or how much information is
there between the actual value

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:53.900
and what we predicted.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:55.200
We want to minimize that.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.210
Another function that you
might have heard in this space

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:58.959
that you might want
to use is like the sum

00:07:58.959 --> 00:08:02.690
of squared errors, which is a
different function, which kind

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:04.106
of works better in this space.

00:08:04.106 --> 00:08:05.480
So, again, it's
a trial and error

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:09.860
to work out what's
good to apply.

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:12.530
So what you're going to do, if
you can imagine that we've--

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:14.180
let's say we
predicted a 6 and we

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:18.080
look at the distance between
6 and the 0.9 and the 1,

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:19.610
that distance is
0.1, but we would

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:21.860
do that for all of
the things that we

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:23.480
predicted it might have been.

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:25.341
And if those distances
are bigger, well,

00:08:25.341 --> 00:08:27.590
that's going to add up, or
it's going to multiply out,

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:30.800
and it's going to be
a worse prediction.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:32.630
The last part of this--
so if you imagine,

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:35.390
we've actually gone through now
like the whole forward pass.

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:38.030
So basically what we've
done, we taken input.

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:40.100
We've multiplied
it by some weights.

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.780
We've done our activations.

00:08:41.780 --> 00:08:43.549
And then we've come
to this function where

00:08:43.549 --> 00:08:46.070
we calculate the loss or the
amount of information that's

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:47.570
left over.

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:49.970
Now we need to go and
basically train our network

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:50.840
and make it better.

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:53.810
So we need to go and work out
what those weights should be.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:57.369
How we do that is backward
propagation and the function--

00:08:57.369 --> 00:08:59.660
basically it's a descent
algorithm, a gradient descent.

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:02.536
So what you'd-- if you imagine
all of those features mapped

00:09:02.536 --> 00:09:04.910
in space, and what we're trying
to do is work out what is

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:06.230
the optimum.

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:07.854
Now we have this nice property.

00:09:07.854 --> 00:09:09.270
We have a gradient
that goes down,

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:09.800
so it has gradient descent.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:12.680
So we're trying to find out
what the global minimum is,

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:14.320
where we have the best fit.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.120
And we will basically retrain
that network over and over

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:20.660
again to work out what the
best set of weights are,

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.760
and that will become
our neural network.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:25.950
So this was super fast,
because the rest of the talk,

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:27.595
I'm going to explain
on architecture

00:09:27.595 --> 00:09:28.970
and how to actually
start getting

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:30.320
this stuff into production.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:33.500
This slide here, you can go
to the GitHub link there.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:36.680
You can also go to the
link here on Google Doc.

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:38.960
And you can find
this whole tutorial,

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:40.850
and you can walk through
it and have a go,

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:42.260
and each of those
steps are explained.

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:44.593
The mathematics behind is
explained a little bit more as

00:09:44.593 --> 00:09:45.300
well.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:49.025
So if that's what you
find most interesting

00:09:49.025 --> 00:09:51.830
and you want to get out
from this, that's a place

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:53.210
I would recommend to start.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:03.350
So machine learning-- what makes
successful machine learning?

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:06.360
It's gonna be fun with
a fan in the moment.

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:07.860
Successful machine
learning, I mean,

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:09.600
as Tino said in the
keynote this morning,

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:11.610
these things have been
around for a long time.

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:14.760
Why are we hearing so much
about it in the last years?

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:18.130
It comes down to storage
and computational power.

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:21.770
So in that space, you know,
we want to have lots of data.

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:23.160
You need lots of storage.

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.470
We want to be able to
actually process this data,

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:27.270
because if we're going to be
doing all this multiplications

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:28.610
and everything with
lots and lots of data

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:30.235
and complicated
networks, there's going

00:10:30.235 --> 00:10:32.580
to be a lot of passes involved.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:35.210
So yeah, we need to
have computational power

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:37.080
to actually work
through our good models.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:41.820
The good model part
is something I've

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:43.800
had a few discussions
with today even.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:47.250
And I'm kind of just like,
OK, what is this democratizing

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:48.180
AI all about?

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:50.940
What is it exactly we're
trying to do in that space?

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:54.150
Well, put your hand up--

00:10:54.150 --> 00:10:56.310
I'm going to keep making
people put their hands up.

00:10:56.310 --> 00:11:01.400
Put your hand up
if you use Linux.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.390
OK, that's a lot more
than the first one.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:07.510
Put your hand up if you actually
contributed to the Linux code

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:09.050
base.

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:11.642
One, two, right, we'll
have a chat later.

00:11:11.642 --> 00:11:13.350
Very interested to
know what you've done.

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:15.829
So my point here is
everyone's using Linux.

00:11:15.829 --> 00:11:17.870
Everyone 's building their
stuff on top of Linux,

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:19.560
yet out of this
whole audience here,

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.643
there are two people in
the audience that actually

00:11:21.643 --> 00:11:23.430
contributed to Linux itself.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:25.204
In the same way, I
think we can start

00:11:25.204 --> 00:11:26.370
looking at machine learning.

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:27.990
That's the space
we're moving into.

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:29.490
There are a lot of
people that spend

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:33.390
a lot of time studying math,
studying machine learning

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:35.670
to actually give us
the tools that we need

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:37.150
to be able to do this stuff.

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:39.675
But what we're seeing now is
more and more people using it

00:11:39.675 --> 00:11:43.240
and more and more people helping
to bring it to production.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:46.410
So our way-- our thinking
along democratizing AI

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:49.650
is to basically bring
that into the same space.

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:52.260
Make sure that everyone
can at some level

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:54.900
start using machine
learning, can

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:56.670
start interacting
with machine learning.

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:57.900
It's going to become--

00:11:57.900 --> 00:11:58.930
I mean, these are
the predictions.

00:11:58.930 --> 00:11:59.710
You're already seeing it now.

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:00.840
We're going to-- in
a few years time,

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:02.631
we're going to have
self-driving cars and--

00:12:02.631 --> 00:12:06.480
I don't know-- telephones
that do everything for us

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:09.460
and make us coffee or whatever
else that they need to do.

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:11.710
So it's going to be like
a huge part of our life.

00:12:11.710 --> 00:12:14.324
I have two children,
and I don't think

00:12:14.324 --> 00:12:16.740
my children are going to grow
up to become mathematicians.

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:19.590
Maybe they will, but
judging by what they're

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:21.540
doing at the moment,
I think the chances

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:23.040
are quite higher
around something

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:25.214
with firefighter and--

00:12:25.214 --> 00:12:26.070
I don't know.

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:28.640
Maybe the other one's
going to be a chef.

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:30.450
They're quite young.

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:31.475
So they can look for--

00:12:31.475 --> 00:12:33.750
but I do want my children
when they grow up

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:35.340
and they're in this
world to not feel

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:37.320
alienated from what's
happening in the world

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:39.030
and how the world works.

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:41.115
So I think
democratizing AI-- this

00:12:41.115 --> 00:12:42.960
is my personal
opinion00 is extremely

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:44.220
important to all of us.

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:46.950
So we should all develop
some understanding

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:49.574
so we can actually understand
what everyone else is

00:12:49.574 --> 00:12:51.990
doing in the world that makes
the world work how it works.

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:57.210
So that's important part
I wanted to bring across.

00:12:57.210 --> 00:12:59.130
Let me grab the clicker again.

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:02.710
So I just told you what we
needed for a good machine

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:06.250
learning, for it
to be successful.

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:11.170
These lovely things here
are our new pods of TPUs.

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:14.970
Each one of these pods
is 11 and 1/2 petaflops.

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:17.580
So that's a whole lot of
capacity to train/ to give some

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:20.749
kind of perspective on that, we
take some of our more difficult

00:13:20.749 --> 00:13:23.040
machine learning models that
we've used for translation

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:24.450
and NLP and this kind of stuff.

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:27.450
When we retrain them,
they were taking days.

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:30.314
With these type of
ASIC devices in a pod

00:13:30.314 --> 00:13:32.730
and set up in this scenario,
we can actually get that down

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:33.540
to hours.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:36.660
So that means that
we can really bring

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:39.404
the kind of real-time
value to what we're trying

00:13:39.404 --> 00:13:40.570
to do with machine learning.

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:41.760
You know, something's
changed in the world.

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:42.759
We need to reflect that.

00:13:42.759 --> 00:13:44.220
We need to retrain our model.

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:47.290
Having this power
enables us to do that.

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:48.540
And cloud is really the only--

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:51.150
I mean, the only space
where we can actually offer

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:52.627
that at scale to everyone.

00:13:52.627 --> 00:13:54.210
Because if you think
how much money it

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:56.580
costs to develop an ASIC
device, I don't know the number,

00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:59.080
but I'm being told that it's
lots and lots and lots and lots

00:13:59.080 --> 00:14:00.420
and lots.

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:02.970
That's not something a company
is going to go and invest in.

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:05.110
But if you want to
train your model

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:08.400
and actually have it play
a part in the real world

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:10.590
when things are changing
and reflect those changes,

00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:15.550
you might want to have access
to this kind of capacity.

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:19.540
So as Peter in a
previous talk mentioned,

00:14:19.540 --> 00:14:23.220
we have two ways that you can
benefit from ML Google Cloud.

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:24.970
One is with APIs.

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:27.495
This is also a good
part of democratizing

00:14:27.495 --> 00:14:28.980
in machine learning as well.

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:30.120
There are common problems.

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:31.786
There are common
capabilities that we're

00:14:31.786 --> 00:14:33.180
developing more and more of.

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:36.090
Being able to share them
over open APIs for anyone,

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:38.634
any developer to
actually implement

00:14:38.634 --> 00:14:40.050
that in their
application to bring

00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:44.066
some new feature, capability
to the application,

00:14:44.066 --> 00:14:44.940
that's a great thing.

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:47.370
Whether it's translating
something or turning speech

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:49.680
into text so you can process
something or understanding

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:51.480
sentiment, that's all great.

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:53.682
And if you're in that
level, you know, good luck.

00:14:53.682 --> 00:14:55.140
It's a really great
space to be in,

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:57.620
and you will go really fast.

00:14:57.620 --> 00:14:59.560
If you need to be
on the left, which

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:04.017
is the building your own
models, we have some offerings

00:15:04.017 --> 00:15:04.600
there as well.

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:07.150
So the two parts that are
really important here,

00:15:07.150 --> 00:15:08.620
one, is TensorFlow.

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:09.950
It's open source.

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:12.760
So if you have ideas, if
you're a data scientist

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:16.134
or if you're a distributed
computer specialist

00:15:16.134 --> 00:15:18.300
and you say I've got ideas
how this can be improved,

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:19.758
you can contribute
to that project.

00:15:19.758 --> 00:15:21.950
You can collaborate
with us on that project.

00:15:21.950 --> 00:15:23.809
It's not just about
Google making something,

00:15:23.809 --> 00:15:24.850
saying come and use this.

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:25.990
It's not ours anymore.

00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:26.710
It's open source.

00:15:26.710 --> 00:15:27.870
It belongs to everyone.

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:30.034
So come to that
space then, right?

00:15:30.034 --> 00:15:32.200
And what we try to do in
Google Cloud-- this is true

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:33.699
of all of our open
source projects--

00:15:33.699 --> 00:15:36.499
is that we provide
a managed service.

00:15:36.499 --> 00:15:38.082
So you can take your
TensorFlow model,

00:15:38.082 --> 00:15:40.165
and we're going to look
at that in a little while,

00:15:40.165 --> 00:15:42.340
and you can bring it to
the Cloud ML service.

00:15:42.340 --> 00:15:43.270
And you can train it.

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:44.470
And you can host it there.

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:46.310
You can see serve from there.

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:48.240
And if you don't like
what it's offering you,

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:51.070
you can just take your model
and go and do it somewhere else.

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:53.590
So that kind of, I can choose
where I'm going to do this;

00:15:53.590 --> 00:15:55.173
I can choose where
I'm going to train;

00:15:55.173 --> 00:15:57.460
I can choose where I'm
going to serve up from,

00:15:57.460 --> 00:15:59.650
that's what we want
from cloud, you know?

00:15:59.650 --> 00:16:01.760
That's the idea of open cloud.

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:05.620
So our job is to make sure that
we do the pricing properly,

00:16:05.620 --> 00:16:09.820
that we offer the best speed,
the best performance, that we

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:10.900
offer the best security.

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:12.910
They're the things that
we need to focus on.

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:15.430
And you can focus on what
that code actually has to do,

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:18.370
and we provide you
a space to run that.

00:16:18.370 --> 00:16:22.750
So this was the architecture
of the video, API demo.

00:16:22.750 --> 00:16:24.550
So for those of you
in the last talk,

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:26.100
you've seen this
quite in depth now.

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:28.347
For those of you from
the keynote this morning,

00:16:28.347 --> 00:16:29.680
just to give you a little taste.

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:32.415
If you were using those APIs,
how simple an architecture

00:16:32.415 --> 00:16:34.600
can be, this is all serverless.

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:36.190
I mean, we have--

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:38.380
the most work that went
into this application

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:42.100
was up here to actually build
what the application should

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:43.570
look like.

00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:47.280
The whole bottom row, let's say
where the magic's happening,

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:49.180
from people uploading
their videos

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:51.715
to a serverless function, a
little JavaScript function,

00:16:51.715 --> 00:16:53.930
it's just basically saying,
oh, you uploaded a video,

00:16:53.930 --> 00:16:55.540
run this API.

00:16:55.540 --> 00:16:58.180
And then the API itself,
that's all servers,

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:00.430
and it's really like
a few lines of codes.

00:17:00.430 --> 00:17:02.471
And then you have that
capability up and running,

00:17:02.471 --> 00:17:04.510
and you're extracting
metadata from your videos.

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:05.947
And you can use
this [? patent ?]

00:17:05.947 --> 00:17:07.780
for any kind of problem
that you might have,

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:11.589
whether it's turning speech
into text or anything else.

00:17:11.589 --> 00:17:13.450
So let's have a little
bit look deeper now

00:17:13.450 --> 00:17:14.800
into what machine running is.

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:16.780
This is one of my
favorite slides.

00:17:16.780 --> 00:17:18.339
It's kind of--
this is what people

00:17:18.339 --> 00:17:20.800
when you have a chat with them
think of machine learning.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:22.841
You know, this is our kind
of common perception--

00:17:22.841 --> 00:17:27.470
tons of data, really difficult
maths and magical results.

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:29.784
And especially that part in
the middle, that's like, oh

00:17:29.784 --> 00:17:31.450
all right, I'm not
really good at maths.

00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:35.956
How much-- what's
my role in this?

00:17:35.956 --> 00:17:37.630
This is closer to what's go--

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:39.220
well, that was too quick.

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:42.142
This is closer to what's
going on in machine learning.

00:17:42.142 --> 00:17:44.350
The big challenges to actually
doing machine learning

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:49.240
start with collecting data, and
that can be a real headache.

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:50.470
I'm a data engineer.

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:51.910
That's my background.

00:17:51.910 --> 00:17:55.750
Finding how to collect data, how
to start organizing that data,

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:57.080
that consumes a lot of time.

00:17:57.080 --> 00:17:58.456
And if you haven't
done that, you

00:17:58.456 --> 00:18:00.538
don't have any data available
for you to go and do

00:18:00.538 --> 00:18:01.600
your machine learning on.

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:03.909
So that's obviously
really important.

00:18:03.909 --> 00:18:05.450
The next part that's
really important

00:18:05.450 --> 00:18:07.150
is creating the model.

00:18:07.150 --> 00:18:09.490
And then once we've got the
model, we need to train it,

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:10.630
we need to make sure
that it's actually

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:12.796
up and running, that the
data does something useful.

00:18:12.796 --> 00:18:15.780
And then we can go
and deploy the model.

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:18.500
This workflow involves
a lot of time.

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:21.360
And I would say each of those
little hexagonals there,

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:24.520
they all involve maybe
not equal amounts of time,

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:27.010
but they all have
their own challenges.

00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:30.340
Today we're not going to
focus on the first two.

00:18:30.340 --> 00:18:33.036
We're going to focus
on the latter three.

00:18:33.036 --> 00:18:35.290
But I'm going to let you see
a little architecture now

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:37.617
of how we might actually
do this in production.

00:18:37.617 --> 00:18:39.700
So when we're talking with
customers, they'll say,

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:41.408
oh, this is the kind
of problem I've got.

00:18:41.408 --> 00:18:42.400
How could I do this?

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:44.636
So the top layer is the--
let's say the input.

00:18:44.636 --> 00:18:47.260
That's what we-- basically when
we're going to do the training,

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:50.680
and the bottom layer is the
predicting, the production

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:51.430
part.

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:54.317
So there are a few
things to notice here.

00:18:54.317 --> 00:18:55.900
We're going to, the
service that we're

00:18:55.900 --> 00:18:59.305
going to for actually collecting
data and extracting them

00:18:59.305 --> 00:19:00.442
are Pub/Sub and Dataflow.

00:19:00.442 --> 00:19:02.650
If you're in the big data
space and you want to hear,

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:04.180
there are talks
about that today.

00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:06.924
Or come by the Instant Insights
little booth out there,

00:19:06.924 --> 00:19:08.340
and we can have a
chat about that.

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:10.060
Or my colleagues as
well can talk to you

00:19:10.060 --> 00:19:11.690
about how they actually work.

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:13.330
In fact, they're doing some
machine learning prediction

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:14.350
there, so they can
really show you

00:19:14.350 --> 00:19:16.210
that whole pipeline
actually playing

00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:19.160
live so you can go and have a
look and see what's going on.

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:21.310
So basically what we
need are capabilities

00:19:21.310 --> 00:19:23.000
to be able to
collect data quickly.

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:26.080
Now Pub/Sub can scale to
millions of message per second.

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:28.720
Or you could have batch
processing if you want there.

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:30.750
Dataflow itself
is a Apache Beam.

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:32.090
It's an open source project.

00:19:32.090 --> 00:19:35.820
And Dataflow will allow you
to actually do that processing

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:38.580
either in streaming or
batch in a unified model.

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:40.720
So you can kind of
treat them the same way.

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:42.970
And then if you think about
what that means, it means,

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:44.710
for example, you could
be training a batch

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:45.700
and doing all your
processing a batch

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:48.430
and extracting of features, but
when it comes to production,

00:19:48.430 --> 00:19:51.010
maybe you're getting similar
data or the same data.

00:19:51.010 --> 00:19:53.000
And you've got to apply
the same kind of logic.

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:54.784
So you could use that
same pipeline now

00:19:54.784 --> 00:19:56.200
applied to your
streaming pipeline

00:19:56.200 --> 00:19:57.612
for the predictions coming in.

00:19:57.612 --> 00:19:59.570
And that's exactly what's
going on in that demo

00:19:59.570 --> 00:20:00.887
out there as well.

00:20:00.887 --> 00:20:02.220
Then we want to train the model.

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:04.800
So Cloud ML has the space
to train that model.

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:07.085
So we can actually go from
Dataflow and say right now,

00:20:07.085 --> 00:20:08.460
let's go and train
the model now.

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:10.200
The data's available,
blah, blah, blah.

00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:12.350
And once we've trained,
it we need to serve it.

00:20:12.350 --> 00:20:14.600
Now Cloud ML also provides
you that place to serve it.

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:16.683
And we'll look at is a
little bit more detail what

00:20:16.683 --> 00:20:18.520
Cloud ML does and
doesn't do in a moment.

00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:22.140
So let's move on to TensorFlow
and spend a few moments

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:23.605
on the architecture
of TensorFlow.

00:20:23.605 --> 00:20:25.230
Just excuse me while
I take some water.

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:33.090
TensorFlow, OK, can
you still hear me?

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:33.930
Yeah, sorry.

00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:34.680
TensorFlow.

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:37.840
It's a system for distributed
parallel machine learning.

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:39.949
Its general purpose--
data flow graphs.

00:20:39.949 --> 00:20:41.490
It has a lot of
words going on there.

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:42.690
What does that all mean?

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:46.120
And it targets
heterogeneous devices.

00:20:46.120 --> 00:20:47.790
What do we mean by
all of those things?

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:50.860
Well, first of all,
this is a framework.

00:20:50.860 --> 00:20:53.910
It's hugely popular, has more
than 50,000 styles on GitHub

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:55.710
for doing machine
learning problems.

00:20:55.710 --> 00:20:57.270
That's a good space to be in.

00:20:57.270 --> 00:20:58.889
Open source, great.

00:20:58.889 --> 00:21:00.930
And there are lots of
people actually using this.

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:03.500
So when you go and you need
to work with other developers

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:05.640
or you need to employ people
to help you develop this model,

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:07.080
you've got a big
community of people

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:08.250
that are already using this.

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:11.380
So that's really important.

00:21:11.380 --> 00:21:14.770
What's a data flow graph
and what kind of devices?

00:21:14.770 --> 00:21:17.250
Well, let's go to the data
flow graph, first of all.

00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:19.290
So just think of
a graph of nodes.

00:21:19.290 --> 00:21:22.289
There are many systems out
there that have graphs of nodes.

00:21:22.289 --> 00:21:24.330
So this should be a common
concept to most people

00:21:24.330 --> 00:21:25.850
I would imagine.

00:21:25.850 --> 00:21:28.992
And basically we're moving from
left to right in this example.

00:21:28.992 --> 00:21:31.200
And we're going to do-- in
those nodes in the middle,

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:32.741
we're going to do
certain operations,

00:21:32.741 --> 00:21:37.820
so matrix multiplication,
activating things,

00:21:37.820 --> 00:21:40.120
the cross entropy, working
out that loss function.

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:43.240
So there are some of the
things we might do there.

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:44.140
So this is--

00:21:44.140 --> 00:21:45.950
OK, nothing special yet.

00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:47.450
The next part that's
really special,

00:21:47.450 --> 00:21:49.658
which the way it starts to
get special is in the name

00:21:49.658 --> 00:21:50.460
itself-- tensors.

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:52.043
So if you come-- if
you have a physics

00:21:52.043 --> 00:21:53.820
background or
mathematics, tensors,

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:55.200
you already know what they are.

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:57.280
It's an N-dimensional array.

00:21:57.280 --> 00:21:58.860
So why is that special?

00:21:58.860 --> 00:22:01.630
Well, think about what an
N-dimensional array is, first

00:22:01.630 --> 00:22:02.130
of all.

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:04.390
So we start with a scalar,
and we move to a vector,

00:22:04.390 --> 00:22:05.473
and then we have a matrix.

00:22:05.473 --> 00:22:07.570
It's kind of like a
table in a spreadsheet.

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:10.980
And then we go on to a
tensor of N dimensions.

00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:13.140
Three dimensions will
provide you a cube.

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:14.880
So you might have
an extra dimension

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:18.680
there, which has something
to do with time, for example.

00:22:18.680 --> 00:22:21.570
Or if you are building
a convolutional network,

00:22:21.570 --> 00:22:24.490
you might have something like
different channels of color.

00:22:24.490 --> 00:22:27.000
So when you're going to do
your convolutional steps,

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:28.590
and what it basically
allows you to do

00:22:28.590 --> 00:22:31.230
is transfer data from
that one node of the graph

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:35.290
to the next node and retain
the shape of the data.

00:22:35.290 --> 00:22:37.600
So if you couldn't retain
the shape of the data,

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:39.210
if you remember in
that first example

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:41.340
where we were
predicting those digits,

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:43.530
because we weren't doing
anything convolutional,

00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:45.780
because we weren't trying
to take those little samples

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:48.180
and actually work out, we lost
basically all of the features.

00:22:48.180 --> 00:22:49.638
So we only had the
grayscale really

00:22:49.638 --> 00:22:51.000
to tell us what was going on.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:52.835
So how many black
pixels were there?

00:22:52.835 --> 00:22:54.210
How many white
pixels were there?

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:55.001
How many gray ones?

00:22:55.001 --> 00:22:58.110
Which of those add up to tell
us that this is that digit?

00:22:58.110 --> 00:22:59.880
But we lost all the
kind of like things

00:22:59.880 --> 00:23:02.760
like the curves or
the texture, you know?

00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:04.752
And obviously, if
you're processing

00:23:04.752 --> 00:23:06.210
more difficult
images, that's going

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:08.350
to be much more important.

00:23:08.350 --> 00:23:10.440
So being able to keep
that data together

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:12.600
as we parse it through
those different node

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:15.450
is really, really important.

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:17.310
The next thing is it has state.

00:23:17.310 --> 00:23:21.030
So we can go and actually update
some of those variables within

00:23:21.030 --> 00:23:24.060
our network, within our--
let's call it our flow--

00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:26.804
and retain the actual
value as we move onward.

00:23:26.804 --> 00:23:28.470
So we can do things
like check pointing,

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:32.380
we can update biases
and that kind of stuff.

00:23:32.380 --> 00:23:34.830
And the last thing
is to distribute it.

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:37.330
Wow, that's a little
tongue twister, isn't it?

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:38.180
Distribute it.

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:40.390
I'm going to get better
at that [CHUCKLING]..

00:23:40.390 --> 00:23:42.451
Ask me when I'm off
stage to say it again.

00:23:42.451 --> 00:23:44.950
So basically we want to say,
well, we've got a lot of nodes.

00:23:44.950 --> 00:23:46.980
We've got a lot of-- we need
a lot of computational power.

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:48.510
How are we going
to split this up?

00:23:48.510 --> 00:23:50.610
So TensorFlow provides
us the architecture

00:23:50.610 --> 00:23:53.700
to actually go and take
those separate pieces of work

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:56.277
that we might want to perform
on one node or multiple nodes

00:23:56.277 --> 00:23:58.110
and go and assign them
to different machines

00:23:58.110 --> 00:24:01.110
or different chipsets.

00:24:01.110 --> 00:24:03.330
So this is kind of
the architecture

00:24:03.330 --> 00:24:05.970
of TensorFlow in one slide.

00:24:05.970 --> 00:24:08.550
The bottom bit, the distribution
and execution engine,

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:10.260
that's where that
magic's all happening.

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:11.820
We've actually--
OK, what chipset am

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:13.240
I going to take advantage of.

00:24:13.240 --> 00:24:15.870
So am I going to use
CPUs on a single machine?

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:17.880
Am I going to use GPUs
on a single machine?

00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:20.340
Am I going to run GPUs
on multiple machines?

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:22.860
Am I going to have some kind
of ASIC device like the TPU,

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:24.160
which I've just discussed?

00:24:24.160 --> 00:24:25.290
So all of those
things, we all think

00:24:25.290 --> 00:24:27.206
we want to be able to
take advantage of those.

00:24:27.206 --> 00:24:30.870
So that TensorFlow execution
engine does that for us.

00:24:30.870 --> 00:24:33.300
And then as we move up,
we have some front ends.

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:36.270
So that's when we start doing
our mathematical or our machine

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:37.450
learning problems.

00:24:37.450 --> 00:24:40.570
And basically, we have a
number of layers in there.

00:24:40.570 --> 00:24:41.670
So the first layer--

00:24:41.670 --> 00:24:44.720
the layers-- is when
you build models.

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:46.470
So the stuff I showed
you at the beginning

00:24:46.470 --> 00:24:48.303
for the MNIST state set
to try and recognize

00:24:48.303 --> 00:24:51.635
those handwritten digits, that
was actually built-in layers.

00:24:51.635 --> 00:24:53.910
And like I said, one of
the things about layers

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:56.340
is that you're going
to spend a lot of time

00:24:56.340 --> 00:24:58.091
wiring your model together.

00:24:58.091 --> 00:25:00.090
And then you're going to
want to take your model

00:25:00.090 --> 00:25:03.160
and run it somewhere else
on a different architecture.

00:25:03.160 --> 00:25:06.060
And you're going to need to make
some quite fundamental changes

00:25:06.060 --> 00:25:07.090
to it.

00:25:07.090 --> 00:25:10.380
So that's a difficult place
to be, and it's a lot of work.

00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:11.400
But maybe you need that.

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:14.310
Maybe there's something that
we don't provide that really

00:25:14.310 --> 00:25:16.590
requires that level of--

00:25:16.590 --> 00:25:18.655
it's also the best place
to go and learn stuff.

00:25:18.655 --> 00:25:20.280
So if you want to
understand it, that's

00:25:20.280 --> 00:25:21.930
why we show it in our CodeLab.

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:23.580
If you go through
a CodeLab there

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:24.810
and you actually do
that stuff, you'll

00:25:24.810 --> 00:25:27.268
really have a good feeling for
what a neural network is all

00:25:27.268 --> 00:25:28.410
about.

00:25:28.410 --> 00:25:31.170
But in production
in our business

00:25:31.170 --> 00:25:32.670
when we're trying
to predict things,

00:25:32.670 --> 00:25:34.961
when we're trying to do
regressions or classifications,

00:25:34.961 --> 00:25:37.800
or when we're trying to process
images or sound, whatever else,

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:40.200
we want to be able to turn
this around a bit quicker.

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:42.760
We want to be able to take
advantage of things quicker.

00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:44.970
So that's why we have
the Estimator level.

00:25:44.970 --> 00:25:47.231
Another popular library
which integrates, as you can

00:25:47.231 --> 00:25:48.480
see, with TensorFlow is Keras.

00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:51.565
So if you're using Keras and
you like Keras, continue.

00:25:51.565 --> 00:25:52.440
It's a great library.

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:54.606
You can take advantage of
the back end of TensorFlow

00:25:54.606 --> 00:25:56.190
to run your models.

00:25:56.190 --> 00:25:58.230
Our Estimator is
at that same level,

00:25:58.230 --> 00:26:00.040
so you can choose
things like, OK,

00:26:00.040 --> 00:26:02.372
I want to do an LSTM
recurrent network,

00:26:02.372 --> 00:26:04.830
and these are the things I
need, and you start configuring.

00:26:04.830 --> 00:26:06.810
And if you do it
in that layer, you

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:09.210
can basically then
say to TensorFlow,

00:26:09.210 --> 00:26:10.620
now run it on the
single machine,

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:13.722
or now let's go and run it in
the cloud on multiple machines.

00:26:13.722 --> 00:26:16.180
So, first of all, you don't
have to do all the hard wiring.

00:26:16.180 --> 00:26:18.300
Second of all, you
get that distribution

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:20.040
and those capabilities back.

00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:22.585
So that's a really
good place to be.

00:26:22.585 --> 00:26:24.600
But if you're, let's
say your problem is

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:27.360
a little bit more commonplace,
like a regression that you want

00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:30.169
to do or a wide and deep network
for some recommendations,

00:26:30.169 --> 00:26:31.710
and you think,
actually, I don't want

00:26:31.710 --> 00:26:33.140
to spend my time
configuring this network;

00:26:33.140 --> 00:26:34.500
I want to take advantage
of a network that's already

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:36.580
out there; I just want
to kind of bring my data

00:26:36.580 --> 00:26:39.300
and see what happens, that's
where the Canned Estimators

00:26:39.300 --> 00:26:40.260
come into place.

00:26:40.260 --> 00:26:41.730
So we're really
trying to make sure

00:26:41.730 --> 00:26:43.800
that regardless of the level,
even if you're actually

00:26:43.800 --> 00:26:46.090
building these models, you
have these different levels

00:26:46.090 --> 00:26:48.925
with which you can come in
and actually start working.

00:26:48.925 --> 00:26:50.814
And on the Estimator
level, it doesn't

00:26:50.814 --> 00:26:52.230
mean because you're
on that level,

00:26:52.230 --> 00:26:54.087
you can't go and
build your own model

00:26:54.087 --> 00:26:56.670
or do something that's different
that hasn't been done before.

00:26:56.670 --> 00:26:57.170
You can.

00:26:57.170 --> 00:26:58.759
We have a Custom
Estimator there.

00:26:58.759 --> 00:27:00.800
So you can actually take
advantage of that Custom

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:03.030
Estimator class and start
building your model there,

00:27:03.030 --> 00:27:05.321
and then use that to run in
a distributed fashion also.

00:27:05.321 --> 00:27:08.070
So Estimators, if you're going
to be doing stuff on TensorFlow

00:27:08.070 --> 00:27:12.990
and you want it in production,
Estimators is a real must.

00:27:12.990 --> 00:27:15.682
So let's have a
look at Cloud ML.

00:27:15.682 --> 00:27:17.890
So we spoke about TensorFlow,
what we're going to do.

00:27:17.890 --> 00:27:19.020
And now we're going
to do some demoing

00:27:19.020 --> 00:27:20.940
and actually look at how we
can get this to production

00:27:20.940 --> 00:27:23.065
and what that actually
means because these are most

00:27:23.065 --> 00:27:24.946
of the questions I
get when people, when

00:27:24.946 --> 00:27:26.070
we're talking to customers.

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:27.750
I built my model in TensorFlow.

00:27:27.750 --> 00:27:29.730
How can I now go and
run it in the cloud?

00:27:29.730 --> 00:27:31.290
Well, let's have a look.

00:27:31.290 --> 00:27:33.630
So I'm just going
to move this fan.

00:27:38.460 --> 00:27:40.069
What we have here is some data.

00:27:40.069 --> 00:27:42.360
And we're going to-- it's
some census data from the US.

00:27:42.360 --> 00:27:44.420
And we're going to use it
to predict income category.

00:27:44.420 --> 00:27:45.330
What does that mean?

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:47.020
We just want to
basically know, this

00:27:47.020 --> 00:27:49.580
is more about the process
and how useful the model is.

00:27:49.580 --> 00:27:51.246
But we want to know
if someone's earning

00:27:51.246 --> 00:27:52.972
more or less than $50,000.

00:27:52.972 --> 00:27:56.890
And we're going to
use Cloud ML for that.

00:27:56.890 --> 00:27:58.770
So the first thing
that we're going to do

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:00.585
is we're going to
run a model locally.

00:28:00.585 --> 00:28:02.460
And I'm going to kick
that off straight away,

00:28:02.460 --> 00:28:04.293
because it's going to
take some time to run.

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:13.110
I did here the low
core I believe.

00:28:16.429 --> 00:28:17.970
While this is running,
we're actually

00:28:17.970 --> 00:28:21.120
going to look at what
the code is doing again.

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:23.740
So what we're doing here,
we're using Cloud ML engine.

00:28:23.740 --> 00:28:26.460
So this is the
API from Cloud ML.

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:30.350
We've built a model
with an Estimator.

00:28:30.350 --> 00:28:33.550
I'm going to show you some code,
what it actually looks like.

00:28:33.550 --> 00:28:35.030
So the important
part here, there

00:28:35.030 --> 00:28:38.597
are two parts when
you're creating this.

00:28:38.597 --> 00:28:40.680
One is the task, what
you're actually going to do.

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:42.013
And the other part is the model.

00:28:42.013 --> 00:28:44.240
So the task is a kind of
orchestration of that.

00:28:44.240 --> 00:28:46.740
And as you can see
in the model here,

00:28:46.740 --> 00:28:48.580
I will scroll down a little bit.

00:28:53.570 --> 00:28:54.860
I want to show you--

00:28:58.979 --> 00:29:00.270
where are we?

00:29:04.662 --> 00:29:06.126
That's not good.

00:29:11.982 --> 00:29:13.850
I'm going to scroll back up now.

00:29:13.850 --> 00:29:14.930
OK, here we go.

00:29:14.930 --> 00:29:16.430
So I basically want
to show you here

00:29:16.430 --> 00:29:18.580
the actual Estimator
that we're returning.

00:29:18.580 --> 00:29:20.780
So this code here, this
is where we're actually

00:29:20.780 --> 00:29:22.830
building our Estimator.

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:24.910
So you can get a kind of feel.

00:29:24.910 --> 00:29:25.640
Zoomed in enough.

00:29:25.640 --> 00:29:27.640
You can get a kind of
feel for the kind of stuff

00:29:27.640 --> 00:29:29.480
that you would be doing there.

00:29:29.480 --> 00:29:30.770
So we were taking layers.

00:29:30.770 --> 00:29:34.316
We're saying how deep it's
going to go, that kind of thing,

00:29:34.316 --> 00:29:35.690
what type of
activation functions

00:29:35.690 --> 00:29:38.130
we're going to be using.

00:29:38.130 --> 00:29:40.700
So this is still running.

00:29:40.700 --> 00:29:42.660
What you're seeing at
the moment is that I just

00:29:42.660 --> 00:29:44.810
installed TensorFlow in a
little Python environment here,

00:29:44.810 --> 00:29:46.143
and I didn't compile TensorFlow.

00:29:46.143 --> 00:29:48.480
So if I was going to use
TensorFlow on my own machine,

00:29:48.480 --> 00:29:51.907
I could actually compile to
take advantage of my hardware.

00:29:51.907 --> 00:29:53.990
And because I haven't done
that, it's just saying,

00:29:53.990 --> 00:29:57.920
well, you could use any of
this hardware, you know?

00:29:57.920 --> 00:30:00.530
The next thing I'm going to
do is launch TensorBoard.

00:30:00.530 --> 00:30:03.872
Now TensorBoard is-- if you're
familiar with like Jupyter

00:30:03.872 --> 00:30:05.330
Notebooks and those
kind of things,

00:30:05.330 --> 00:30:07.850
you might use something
like matplotlib to actually

00:30:07.850 --> 00:30:11.300
kind of graph out and
see what's going on.

00:30:11.300 --> 00:30:14.942
And again, it's like
an epiphany moment.

00:30:14.942 --> 00:30:17.150
So TensorBoard will allow
us to do that kind of stuff

00:30:17.150 --> 00:30:19.721
and much more, but in
our distributed system.

00:30:19.721 --> 00:30:21.470
And so if we're running
this in the cloud,

00:30:21.470 --> 00:30:23.540
we're running it locally, we
can actually take advantage

00:30:23.540 --> 00:30:24.595
and see what's going on.

00:30:24.595 --> 00:30:25.470
So let's launch that.

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:37.590
And I believe the log
there is a census output.

00:30:44.676 --> 00:30:46.175
And I'm going to
allow this process.

00:30:53.430 --> 00:30:54.900
That's not good.

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:08.270
And I can actually
now come in here

00:31:08.270 --> 00:31:12.110
and look up how
accurate my model is.

00:31:12.110 --> 00:31:12.830
I can look at--

00:31:12.830 --> 00:31:14.106
so here's the accuracy.

00:31:14.106 --> 00:31:15.980
I can start looking at
the loss of the model.

00:31:15.980 --> 00:31:18.650
So you can see the
training went OK.

00:31:18.650 --> 00:31:21.080
Evaluation was pretty poor.

00:31:21.080 --> 00:31:23.310
But what I can
also do is go here

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:24.780
and actually-- so
bearing in mind,

00:31:24.780 --> 00:31:26.624
we've just used
an Estimator, you

00:31:26.624 --> 00:31:28.790
might be interested in
actually, OK, what's actually

00:31:28.790 --> 00:31:29.900
going on in that network?

00:31:29.900 --> 00:31:31.026
What have I actually built?

00:31:31.026 --> 00:31:32.983
So it will give you the
ability to come in here

00:31:32.983 --> 00:31:33.970
and actually look.

00:31:33.970 --> 00:31:35.120
And it's going to be super
