00:40:25.600 --> 00:40:27.840
A few things to mention before--

00:40:27.840 --> 00:40:30.370
the Cloud ML service has some
other properties as well.

00:40:30.370 --> 00:40:31.870
So one of the things
that you can do

00:40:31.870 --> 00:40:33.610
is hyperparameter tuning.

00:40:33.610 --> 00:40:35.152
So if you're using
an Estimator, some

00:40:35.152 --> 00:40:36.859
of the things that
you might want to have

00:40:36.859 --> 00:40:37.727
is hyperparameters.

00:40:37.727 --> 00:40:39.310
So these are basically
just parameters

00:40:39.310 --> 00:40:41.860
that you're passing
externally to your model.

00:40:41.860 --> 00:40:44.400
It might be, for example, OK,
I'm doing a recurrent network,

00:40:44.400 --> 00:40:46.370
and I want to go to
so many levels deep.

00:40:46.370 --> 00:40:49.757
So you might say, the number
of levels deep is so far.

00:40:49.757 --> 00:40:51.340
Or you're doing a
learning rate that's

00:40:51.340 --> 00:40:53.923
kind of like, I showed you that
gradient descent when you were

00:40:53.923 --> 00:40:55.150
trying to actually optimize.

00:40:55.150 --> 00:40:56.989
And basically on
the gradient step,

00:40:56.989 --> 00:40:58.780
you're taking little
steps down to find out

00:40:58.780 --> 00:40:59.946
where the global minimum is.

00:40:59.946 --> 00:41:02.080
And if you imagine trying
to traverse a valley,

00:41:02.080 --> 00:41:03.660
if you were taking
two big steps,

00:41:03.660 --> 00:41:05.800
you might just jump
from side to side.

00:41:05.800 --> 00:41:07.630
And if you were taking
two small steps,

00:41:07.630 --> 00:41:09.752
it would take forever
or take a long time.

00:41:09.752 --> 00:41:11.710
So your learning rate--
and you can apply decay

00:41:11.710 --> 00:41:14.380
to that as well-- is saying
right, we'll take a big step

00:41:14.380 --> 00:41:17.860
and then slightly less and then
decrease the size of the steps

00:41:17.860 --> 00:41:20.450
as we see that that change is
having less and less effect

00:41:20.450 --> 00:41:21.070
into our actual functions.

00:41:21.070 --> 00:41:22.600
There's a way of
optimizing that.

00:41:22.600 --> 00:41:25.720
So all of these things to work
out what the best learning

00:41:25.720 --> 00:41:28.660
rate is to work out
how many levels deep we

00:41:28.660 --> 00:41:30.290
want to create our
neural network.

00:41:30.290 --> 00:41:33.215
These are things-- if you're
going to do this by yourself,

00:41:33.215 --> 00:41:35.590
you would have to go and
retrain your model and rerun it.

00:41:35.590 --> 00:41:37.340
So basically high
performance opportunity.

00:41:37.340 --> 00:41:40.330
It's basically saying expose
those things as parameters

00:41:40.330 --> 00:41:42.340
and then use the
service to say I want

00:41:42.340 --> 00:41:43.850
to spend so much on my model.

00:41:43.850 --> 00:41:45.340
So it's like, this is the range.

00:41:45.340 --> 00:41:48.270
I'm going to go between
two levels deep and 10.

00:41:48.270 --> 00:41:50.590
I'm going to go from a learning
rate from this to that.

00:41:50.590 --> 00:41:53.641
And then you basically say
to Cloud ML, go and run this,

00:41:53.641 --> 00:41:56.140
and you create a configuration
file with that configuration.

00:41:56.140 --> 00:41:57.514
And it will
basically try out all

00:41:57.514 --> 00:41:59.890
those different configurations,
and it will come back.

00:41:59.890 --> 00:42:01.309
And it doesn't
just go willy-nilly

00:42:01.309 --> 00:42:02.350
and try all those things.

00:42:02.350 --> 00:42:04.150
It will work out,
OK, I did this.

00:42:04.150 --> 00:42:05.740
That doesn't look
so good, so maybe

00:42:05.740 --> 00:42:07.690
if I take a step in this
direction, that looks better.

00:42:07.690 --> 00:42:09.148
Let's keep going
in that direction.

00:42:09.148 --> 00:42:12.500
So work out what the best set of
parameters are for your model.

00:42:12.500 --> 00:42:14.864
So you'll get the best
working model as well.

00:42:14.864 --> 00:42:16.780
So the model that trains
the fastest, but also

00:42:16.780 --> 00:42:18.550
the model that has the most
predictive power, the most

00:42:18.550 --> 00:42:19.510
accuracy.

00:42:19.510 --> 00:42:20.459
So that can really--

00:42:20.459 --> 00:42:22.000
that's another
headache that you have

00:42:22.000 --> 00:42:23.180
when you're trying to
train these things when

00:42:23.180 --> 00:42:24.190
there's a certain scale.

00:42:24.190 --> 00:42:25.690
It's because like,
what is the best?

00:42:25.690 --> 00:42:28.260
Especially if you're in this
customer level, this custom

00:42:28.260 --> 00:42:30.010
Estimator level, and
you're trying to say,

00:42:30.010 --> 00:42:32.019
what is the best set
of configuration?

00:42:32.019 --> 00:42:33.810
So Cloud ML actually
allows you to do that.

00:42:33.810 --> 00:42:35.768
It will go and do that
experimentation for you.

00:42:35.768 --> 00:42:37.960
And you can decide how
much bandwidth you give it

00:42:37.960 --> 00:42:40.780
or how much money you
want to spend to work out.

00:42:40.780 --> 00:42:42.790
So you can say this
is worth this much

00:42:42.790 --> 00:42:44.560
to me, because I
want this accuracy.

00:42:44.560 --> 00:42:46.434
Or it's not.

