00:42:57.760 --> 00:42:59.440
they're going to show some
of the big data tools.

00:42:59.440 --> 00:43:01.230
And I asked and I wasn't sure
if they're going to show Datalab

00:43:01.230 --> 00:43:01.900
or not.

00:43:01.900 --> 00:43:05.920
But Datalab is basically a
managed Jupyter Notebook.

00:43:05.920 --> 00:43:10.010
If you go to our cloud
console, and you basically--

00:43:10.010 --> 00:43:11.830
you can go in there
and you use Cloud Shell

00:43:11.830 --> 00:43:14.800
and just do "datalab
create" and give it a name,

00:43:14.800 --> 00:43:16.640
it will spin you up an instance.

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:18.700
You can give it a
machine type as well

00:43:18.700 --> 00:43:21.387
if you think I need more power
than the standard machine type.

00:43:21.387 --> 00:43:23.470
And basically what you'll
get is your own instance

00:43:23.470 --> 00:43:25.530
of a Jupyter Notebook running.

00:43:25.530 --> 00:43:29.240
It's connected with, well it has
Ungit running on there as well.

00:43:29.240 --> 00:43:31.240
So you can connect with
a GitHub repository

00:43:31.240 --> 00:43:35.560
or you can connect with the
Git repository that's actually

00:43:35.560 --> 00:43:37.300
included in the cloud project.

00:43:37.300 --> 00:43:38.560
So you can share that
with multiple people

00:43:38.560 --> 00:43:39.472
across your project.

00:43:39.472 --> 00:43:41.930
If you have a Jupyter Notebook,
everything installed there.

00:43:41.930 --> 00:43:44.080
So like TensorFlow's
there, it's up to date,

00:43:44.080 --> 00:43:45.384
all the stuff you need.

00:43:45.384 --> 00:43:47.050
So all the kind of
good Python libraries

00:43:47.050 --> 00:43:49.720
that you might be using, but
also the integration with tools

00:43:49.720 --> 00:43:52.720
like BigQuery and Google
Cloud Storage are all there.

00:43:52.720 --> 00:43:55.240
So basically by
using Datalab, you

00:43:55.240 --> 00:43:57.984
have an environment you can
spin up in just a few minutes,

00:43:57.984 --> 00:44:00.400
and it will give you everything
you need to actually start

00:44:00.400 --> 00:44:03.390
building these models
and experimenting them

00:44:03.390 --> 00:44:04.549
within Google Cloud.

00:44:04.549 --> 00:44:07.090
So that's why I included this
slide, just as a callout so you

00:44:07.090 --> 00:44:08.923
